; A fixed FLOAT arm in a choice has no member key to name its enum variant after, so the variant
; name is minted from the value's LEXEME (`1.5` -> `F1.5`) — which is not a Rust identifier. That
; name used to be emitted verbatim and die at rustfmt with an error about rustfmt's own confusion
; (`expected item, found 5`), naming neither the arm nor a way out, so these must reject GRACEFULLY
; at parse instead. Sanitized auto-naming (`F1_5`) is deliberately NOT what this does: a variant
; name is public API of the generated crate, so the author picks it via `; @name`.
; Every arm-naming path onto that minter is pinned here:
;   - `a`  bare rule-level type choice (create_variants_from_type_choices).
;   - `b`  all-fixed c-style enum — same minted variants, one rejection per offending arm.
;   - `c`  nested ANONYMOUS choice: no rule owns the arms (role-generic wording), and the enclosing
;          rule ident is minted from the same lexeme (`F1.5OrText`), so the refusal has to land
;          before generation rather than at the variant alone.
;   - `d`  bare-member GROUP choice — a different consumer (the group-choice arm loop's fallback),
;          reached only when the member has no key.
; Green neighbours, kept here so a future refactor that over-widens the predicate shows up as a
; catalog flip rather than silently: `e` names the arm explicitly (the supported route), and `f`
; is the NAMED-member group-choice spelling, whose variant comes from the member key `v`, not the
; lexeme. The predicate is on the minted STRING, not on the value's kind, so a fixed uint or text
; arm (`5`, `"x"`) is untouched — see choice_nint_arm_variant_name.cddl for the nint kind.
a = 1.5 / tstr
b = 1.5 / 2.5
c = [1.5 / tstr]
d = [ true // 1.5 ]
e = 1.5 ; @name half
  / tstr
f = [ true // v: 1.5 ]
